   6c. Group scope, group cycling and connected-only for list sessions. All
       three narrow a list through the one mechanism 6b built: the list's
       VIEW_SOURCE.shown (today NULL). Predicates compose.
       CONTRACT. A context can declare the group it stands for in lists:
       DATA_CAP_GROUP + DataOps.group_key(user_data), 0 = none, with the
       usual app_intrf/ui_layer pass-through. app_data implements it for LV2
       and CLAP plugin instances, synth oscillators and the sampler, returning
       exactly what root_port_group_key gives their ports. Root does NOT
       implement it. DataChoice.group_key's doc changes from "NOT resolvable
       as a ContextId" to "matches the group a context declares, if any" -
       a key is still never turned into a context.
       SCOPE. Root's actions become a third action source beside current and
       hovered (not gathered twice when current or hovered already is root),
       so Connect is reachable from anywhere. When a session starts, walk up
       from the hovered context: the first context whose declared group
       appears among the rows of the session's FIRST list scopes that list;
       none found = unfiltered. For CONNECT that is the source list only - the
       peers are almost always somewhere else. The rule never mentions ports,
       root, or how ids are composed. Cost: "Connect" and a param's "Choose"
       both take `C`, so hovering a param with choices costs one extra drill
       keypress.
       CYCLING. `g` steps the active list's filter through all -> each
       distinct group (by group_label, in list order) -> all, on either list.
       This is what reaches foreign clients, which no context can scope. The
       contextual scope is simply where the source list's cycle starts, so
       cycling to "all" is how scope is dropped - no separate key. Fewer than
       two groups = `g` does nothing. The group set is rebuilt from the rows
       when `g` is pressed; if the active group has no rows left (its ports
       went away mid-session) the filter falls back to all.
       CONNECTED ONLY. `c` on the peers list toggles a DATA_CHOICE_LINKED
       predicate, composed with the group filter. The one piece of the dropped
       6d worth keeping: "what is this port wired to" becomes one keypress
       instead of scanning the peers for [connected].
       The title names the active filter, e.g. "Port (Reverb)", "Connect to
       (system, connected)". Filter state lives per list in the LIST_SESSION,
       next to the cursors.
       Two things 6b left for this step, because an unfiltered list never hits
       them:
       * helper_view_build draws NOTHING when the cursor sits on an item that
         is not shown, so the list cursor must always be on a shown row -
         including right after the filter changes. helper_list_cursor_anchor's
         gone-row fallback is "row 0", which may itself be filtered out; it
         has to become "nearest shown row".
       * helper_list_cursor_move wraps by list_count and never skips anything.
         It needs the step-over-hidden walk helper_child_visible_step already
         does for contexts - put that walk (and _nearest) on VIEW_SOURCE, so
         contexts and lists share it the way they share the window.
       list_count is the UNFILTERED count, so "is there more beyond the
       window" must come from the traversal's own walk - do not later
       optimise it into a count lookup.
       VERIFY: hovering a plugin, Connect opens on only its ports; at root it
       opens on all. `g` reaches system/other apps and comes back to all. `c`
       shows exactly the [connected] peers. Removing the scoped plugin
       mid-session drops the list back to all. Window markers stay honest
       under every filter.
